p to plan you  know we we have Parcels automation that  was less than 30 percent uh less than  two years ago we're now at the 70  percent uh Mark and I was at our super  Hub in the Midlands as well which means  we can really compete and win in the  parcels market and that was looking  magnificent as well and that will come  on stream in the summer so what I am  really focused on is what's ahead of us  and what I would say is that our  customer experience on the doorstep  remains really great the citizens advice  uh did a survey last week and we are  number one versus our competitors and a  big thank you to our team for making  that happen on the doorstep as well our  infrastructure is coming on stream  exactly as we planned the problem to  solve is the ways of working and we are  happy to pay for that change and that's  why we have offered a 400 million pounds  worth of nine percent pay increase over  the next 18 months now your parent  company International distribution  services has been talking increasingly  about spinning off GLS which is the very  profitable International Parcels  business what sort of Future Would a  standalone Royal Mail have under those  circumstances  well Ian what I'm focused on is turning  around Royal Mail in the UK and you know  I said it very very clearly last year at  a half year results that I will do and  we will do whatever it takes  we did say uh back at our AGM earlier on  in the year that of course all options  are open and we won't cross subsidize  between GLS our international business  and Royal Mail in the UK but look I'm  focused on the turnaround and what we  need is the change to win and compete in  the market and I've always been  confident that we can do just that our  customer experience is great our  infrastructure is coming on stream what  we need is the ways of working to  reflect that and we've always said we're  happy to pay for it for that and that is  exactly what our deal is about well  something else that could really uh  benefit you is a watering down of the  universal service obligation which  obviously costs you a great deal of  money I mean that's in the gift of the  government what doesn't the government  get about the need to uh alter the terms  of the Uso  well in terms of the Uso there are two  key criterias that need to be met one is  that it's financially viable and one  that it meets the needs of the customers  I think if we start with the needs of  the customers I mean ofcom did their own  review there's some time back that said  that a five-day letter service would  meet 97  um of you know customers needs it's also  worth bearing in mind and I think we can  all see that in terms of our own  letterboxes but in 2004 2005 we  delivered 20 billion letters a year it's  now 8 billion and actually during covert  the letter decline was around about 25  percent  and what the customers also say is they  want a seven day parcel business I think  in terms of the financial viability side  of stuff you know we're losing a million  pounds a day this year we're forecast to  lose between 350 and 450 million pounds  a day what we want to do is make sure  we're doing the right thing for the  customers which is a seven day Parcel  Service and make sure that's what we're  investing in and we're spending our time  also on what customers want and not on  what customers no longer want  all right so I mean Before I Let You Go  um should I be posting any presents that  I buy online now in order to them having  have giving them a chance of getting to  my loved ones by Christmas Day well Ian  I feel very sad to say what I'm going to  say and what I would you know be clear  is please go to our websites and and  have a look at the latest information  and we will do everything that we can  and a big thank you to our  non-operational managers who are out and  about delivering today but I would say  to everyone please post early for  Christmas  now Simon no we spoke to Dave Ward from  the CW earlier I mean you talked about  uh having held discussions with them  over the weekend he's he told us on Sky  news this morning he'd be prepared to  meet with you today or tomorrow is that  possible  and we are always available to talk but  I just want to make it really clear that  we have been talking intensively over  the last three weeks with the help of  acas  um including all of the weekends and we  have now made our final offer so what I  would really encourage the CWU to do and  actually the CW leadership to do is  really take the chance now to reflect  and reflect on saving Christmas and  their members jobs and I really hope  that they make the right decision
 

